# Lesson 6, Mike's Exercise 5, turned so the student (South) is on lead.
title: Your Exercise 5: a hand like a foot
id: L06-07
type: exercise
lesson: 6
concepts: 6.6, 6.1
student: S
board: 7

deal:            # spades  hearts  diamonds  clubs
  N  765    AKT84  63      K82
  E  AQJ9   532    KQT4    AT
  S  8432   Q7     J52     9643
  W  KT     J96    A987    QJ75

auction:
  S Pass   = Not enough to open
  W Pass   = Not enough to open
  N Pass   = Not enough to open
  E 1NT    = 15-17 points, balanced
  S Pass   = Nothing to say
  W 3NT    = 10-14 points, balanced, no 4-card major: let's play game
  N Pass   = Nothing to say
  E Pass   = Happy with game
  S Pass   = Nothing to add

auction recap:
  After three passes, East opened 1NT and West raised straight to 3NT. West
  didn't use Stayman, so West has no four-card major. East is declarer, and
  so you, South, will lead to the first trick.

lead planning:
  declarer: 15-17 = East's 1NT showed 15 to 17.
  dummy: 10-14 = West bid game opposite 1NT: 10 to 14. (West passed at first, so 12 at most is likely.)
  partner: 6-12 = 40, minus your 3, East's 15 to 17 and West's 10 to 14, leaves 6 to 12.
  remind: You have almost nothing. Where are your side's tricks going to come from?
  remind: West skipped Stayman. What does that tell you about the majors?

play:
  1   S HQ*  W H6  N H4  E H2
      HQ key: Your own suits have no future, so look for partner's. Dummy skipped Stayman, so West has no four-card major, and you hold four spades: partner very likely has hearts. From a doubleton, lead the honor.
      HQ not H7: Right suit! From a doubleton, lead the honor first, so your queen can't block partner's suit later.
      HQ not S: You hold four spades yourself, so partner probably doesn't have many. Which major is partner more likely to hold?
      HQ not D: Your diamonds have no high cards. Even if you set them up, you'd never get in to cash them. Look for partner's suit.
      HQ not C: Your clubs have no high cards. Even if you set them up, you'd never get in to cash them. Look for partner's suit.
      note: Partner plays low and your queen holds the trick.
  2   S H7  W H9  N HT  E H3
      note: Now your second heart. Partner's 10 beats dummy's 9.
  3   N HA  E H5  S C3  W HJ
      note: Partner cashes the ♥A, and dummy's jack falls.
  4   N HK  E CT  S C4  W C5
      note: Partner's hearts are all winners now.
  5   N H8  E D4  S S2  W C7
      note: Five tricks for the defense: 3NT is down one.
  6   N D6  E DK  S D2  W D7
      note: Partner gets out with a diamond. Declarer takes the rest.
  7   E DQ  S D5  W D8  N D3
  8   E DT  S DJ  W DA  N C2
  9   W D9  N C8  E S9  S C6
  10  W SK  N S5  E SA  S S3
  11  E SQ  S S4  W ST  N S6
  12  E SJ  S S8  W CJ  N S7
  13  E CA  S C9  W CQ  N CK

play recap:
  Your hand had no suit worth setting up, so you led for partner's. The
  clues were all there: West had no four-card major, you held four spades,
  and partner held most of the defense's points. The ♥Q found partner's
  hearts, and the defense ran five heart tricks before declarer could take
  nine. Any other lead would have given declarer time to make 3NT. Leading
  the honor from your doubleton mattered too: it kept your hearts from
  getting in partner's way.
